t it as valid per instructions).\n\nStill, raising by only 100 may look weak or like a float attempt. Going all-in (-2) would be a large overplay (~19,500), which is too much for a semi-bluff unless you think your opponent will fold frequently — unlikely given their pre-flop aggression.\n\nThus, promising moves:\n- **Check (0):** Low-risk, see the turn cheaply. But misses value and lets opponent realize equity.\n- **Raise to 100:** Too small, might invite calls from worse draws or marginal hands, but looks suspicious.\n- **Raise to ~800–1000:** Ideal size (pot-sized or 2/3 pot), builds the pot while representing strength. Since any value >100 is allowed, this is feasible.\n- **All-in (-2):** Overkill, turns your hand into a bluff, risks too much for little fold equity.\n\nBest choice: **Raise by 800** — balances risk/reward, represents strength (possibly a completed flush or straight already), and puts pressure on middle pairs or weak aces.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong draw with high equity (~36% to hit flush or straight), and your opponent has shown hesitation post-flop. A moderate semi-bluff raise builds the pot when you\'re likely ahead in equity and may win the pot immediately. Checking surrenders initiative. A 100 raise is too small to be meaningful. An 800-chip raise is credible and within chip limits.\n\nAnswer: 800'}
