t or force weaker hands/draws out.\n- **Larger raise**: Could be good for protection or value, but risks scaring away worse hands that might call smaller bets.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill? You have ~18,900 chips left. Going all-in would commit most of your stack. Risky unless you\'re committed to the draw.\n\nBest move: **Make a moderate raise** to price out draws and gain information. Since the minimum raise is 100 (and must be at least double previous bet if there was one), but no bet was made this round — opponent checked. So you can check or raise any amount ≥ big blind equivalent (but 100 is allowed as per valid actions).\n\nBut note: "valid actions" include 100 as a raise option, so likely the minimum bet is 100.\n\nRaising by **100** is small but acceptable. However, given the pot size (unclear exactly, but likely around 200 + 800 + 800 = at least 1800?), a 100 raise is tiny — may not deter anyone.\n\nBut your goal is to maximize EV. Since you have high equity and initiative, **betting for value and protection** makes sense.\n\nHowever, the valid actions suggest that **100 is the smallest possible raise** — so likely the minimum increment. Raising 100 serves as a **blocking bet** or **probe**, but doesn’t commit.\n\nAlternatively, going **all-in (-2)** might be too exploitative. Opponent could just call with a better hand or fold worse.\n\nThus, **raising by 100** is safe, keeps the initiative, and tests the waters.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong draw with multiple outs (straight, flush, overcards), and your opponent has shown consistent passivity. A small raise of 100 protects your equity slightly and builds the pot while likely getting called by worse hands or draws. It’s low risk and maintains pressure. Checking gives a free card, which could let opponent hit a winning hand.\n\nAnswer: 100'}
